      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/da2336d6c4360d10fedbfc6441280b5a08091df34f3f59e2e94e7d4daf01bc3f.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Square\n\nII. Trapezoid\n\nIII. Rhombus",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral where all sides are congruent and all angles are right, so it is a square.\n\n- Since the quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to each other, not one pair, it is **not** a trapezoid.\n\n- The quadrilateral is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. So it is a rhombus.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (square)  and III (rhombus).",
