    "q-128291": {
      "dom_id": "step-q128291",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/94a6607f6d12c3c59c9822d91dfb6c6b8b3a8d23bba9ef1c3a7253be564f95d8.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. There is a pair of opposite sides that are not parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since every pair of opposite sides in a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite in which no pair of opposite sides are parallel, so it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $Q$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shapes $P$ and $S$ are not parallelograms.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
