    "q-128251": {
      "dom_id": "step-q128251",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/781e1e9f5a08cf7f01d9896e957f8d983cd428817e3a5eeb90312a89fdc0a932.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rhombus?",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a  rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.  \n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. The sides are not all congruent, so it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a kite. Since the sides are not all congruent, it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus since all its sides are congruent and the opposite sides are parallel.\n\n- The shape $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid.  Since it has only one pair of congruent sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\nTherefore, only shape $R$ is a rhombus.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
