    "q-53060": {
      "dom_id": "step-q53060",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A square is always a kite.\n\nII. A rectangle is always a square.\n\nIII. A rhombus is always a trapezoid.",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14151358/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A square fits the description because all the sides in a square are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is false. In a square, all the sides are congruent. However, in a rectangle, it's only necessary that two pairs of opposite sides are congruent to each other. Therefore, a rectangle is not always a square.\n\n- Statement III is false. In a trapezoid, only $1$ pair of opposite sides are parallel to each other. However, in a rhombus, the $2$ pairs of opposite sides are parallel to each other. Therefore, a rhombus is not a trapezoid.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III only",
              "html": "III only"
            },
            {
              "option": "b",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-b",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II only",
              "html": "II only"
            },
            {
              "option": "c",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-c",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and III only",
              "html": "I and III only"
            },
            {
              "option": "d",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-d",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II and III only",
              "html": "II and III only"
            },
            {
              "option": "e",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-e",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I only",
              "html": "I only"
            }
          ],
          "choices_complete": true
        }
      ],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "proof_feedback": null,
      "html": "<div id=\"step-q53060\" class=\"step questionWidget\" style=\"display: block;\"><div class=\"questionWidget-header\"><div class=\"questionWidget-title\">Question 6</div><div class=\"questionWidget-helpButton\">?</div></div><div class=\"questionWidget-text\"><p>Which of the following statements are true?</p>\n<ol class=\"questionStatements\"><li>  A square is always a kite. </li><li>  A rectangle is always a square. </li><li>  A rhombus is always a trapezoid. </li></ol></div><table class=\"questionWidget-choicesTable\"><tbody><tr><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetter\"><div id=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-a\" class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle\">a</div></td><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceText\">III only</td></tr><tr><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetter\"><div id=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-b\" class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle\">b</div></td><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceText\">II only</td></tr><tr><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetter\"><div id=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-c\" class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle\">c</div></td><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceText\">I and III only</td></tr><tr><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetter\"><div id=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-d\" class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle\">d</div></td><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceText\">II and III only</td></tr><tr><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetter\"><div id=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-53060-e\" class=\"questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle\" style=\"background: rgb(64, 64, 64); color: white;\">e</div></td><td class=\"questionWidget-choiceText\">I only</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"questionWidget-buttonBar\"><div class=\"questionWidget-submitButton disabledButton\" style=\"display: none;\">Submit</div></div><div class=\"questionWidget-spinnerFrame\"><div class=\"questionWidget-spinner\" style=\"display: block;\"></div></div><div class=\"questionWidget-result\"><div class=\"questionWidget-correctIncorrectIcon\"><img src=\"/img/correct-answer.svg\"></div><div class=\"questionWidget-correctText\">Correct</div></div><div class=\"questionWidget-explanationHeader\">EXPLANATION</div><div class=\"questionWidget-explanation\"><p>First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.</p>\n<div id=\"graphic-q-53060-e-0\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/q-53060-e-0\"></div>\n\n<p>Let's now look at each statement in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A square fits the description because all the sides in a square are congruent.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement II is false. In a square, all the sides are congruent. However, in a rectangle, it's only necessary that two pairs of opposite sides are congruent to each other. Therefore, a rectangle is not always a square.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is false. In a trapezoid, only <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"29\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> pair of opposite sides are parallel to each other. However, in a rhombus, the <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"30\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> pairs of opposite sides are parallel to each other. Therefore, a rhombus is not a trapezoid.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In conclusion, only statement I is true.</p>\n</div></div>",
